/* Original companion notes for Beginning ReactJS Foundations, in source order. */
window.TEXTBOOK_UNITS=window.TEXTBOOK_UNITS||{};
window.TEXTBOOK_UNITS.react=[
['Map the prerequisites, installation examples, reader resources, and scope before building a component. The original setup examples describe the book’s 2022 toolchain.','Development toolchain','The tools that resolve dependencies, transform source, serve a development build, and produce deployable assets.','Why separate React from its development toolchain?','React describes and updates user interfaces. A package manager, bundler, transpiler, development server, and hosting system solve different problems. A toolchain can change while component and state concepts remain useful. Identify which tool owns each error before changing application code.'],
['Compare a directly loaded React example with a JSX build workflow, then trace an input change into displayed output.','React root','The entry point through which React manages a component tree inside a host container.','What happens between a component definition and a visible interface?','The application creates a root, supplies an element describing the component tree, and React renders and commits the necessary host changes. JSX must be transformed if the chosen runtime cannot parse it. A component definition alone does not mount an interface.'],
['Connect the UI layer, declarative rendering, component composition, and the purpose and limits of React.','Declarative UI','An interface description derived from current inputs and state, leaving the renderer to perform the host updates.','Why does declarative rendering help when UI state changes?','Describe the view for the current state instead of manually keeping multiple DOM mutations consistent. React calls components to obtain a new description and reconciles it with the existing tree. This does not remove the need for correct state design, stable identity, or side-effect management.'],
['Read JSX as JavaScript syntax for element descriptions: expressions, attributes, children, fragments, conditions, and transforms.','JSX','A JavaScript syntax extension transformed into element-creation operations; it is neither HTML nor a template executed directly by a browser.','How can a JSX conditional accidentally display a zero?','With count && <Badge />, JavaScript evaluates the expression to zero when count is zero, and React can render that number. Use an explicit boolean such as count > 0 or a conditional expression returning null. Explain expression evaluation before blaming the JSX transform.'],
['Compare elements and component definitions, built-in and custom components, classes and functions, props, children, and composition.','Component','A reusable unit that describes part of the interface from inputs and, where needed, its own state.','How do an element and a component differ?','A component is a function or class defining behavior for a piece of UI. An element is a description of what should appear, including the component type and props. Calling the same component at different tree positions can create independent stateful instances; stable identity and keys control preservation.'],
['Use component inspection and profiling to follow props, state, renders, and likely sources of unnecessary work.','React Profiler','A tool for measuring render commits and identifying which parts of a component tree contribute to rendering cost.','How would you investigate a slow React interaction?','Reproduce the interaction, record a profile, inspect costly commits and changing inputs, then test one targeted improvement. A render is not automatically a DOM update, and a render count alone is not a user-facing performance measure. Verify the change under a representative workload.'],
['Trace one-way data flow, props, state, lifting state, immutable updates, reducers, and shared state ownership.','State snapshot','The values observed by a particular render and by handlers created during that render.','Why can three setCount(count + 1) calls produce only one increment?','All three calls read the same count snapshot and enqueue the same replacement value. When each update depends on the pending value, use setCount(c => c + 1); three queued updater functions then compose to add three. Do not mutate existing state objects to force a render.'],
['Connect event names, handler references, event objects, default behavior, propagation, and event-driven state updates.','Event propagation','The traversal of an event through related nodes and handlers, with capture and bubble phases where supported.','How do preventDefault and stopPropagation differ?','preventDefault requests cancellation of a cancelable browser default such as submitting a form. stopPropagation prevents further traversal to other nodes. Neither substitutes for the other. Prefer a semantic button or form and handle the correct event instead of reproducing browser behavior with a generic element.'],
['Compare controlled and uncontrolled inputs, input types, submission, validation, and maintaining a consistent source of truth.','Controlled input','An input whose displayed value or checked state is driven by component state and updated through a change handler.','Why must a controlled input update its backing value on change?','React renders the supplied value. If the handler does not update the backing state consistently, the next render restores the old value and typing appears blocked. Use value with onChange, checked for checkboxes, and an explicit initial value. Keep validation and accessible error messages separate from raw input capture.'],
['Use refs to retain mutable values and interact with DOM elements without treating a ref mutation as a render request.','Ref','A stable object with a mutable current field that can hold a value across renders without itself triggering a re-render.','When should a value be state instead of a ref?','If changing the value should change the rendered output, use state. Use a ref for an imperative DOM handle or a mutable value not used to derive the visible UI. A focus action can use a DOM ref after commit; writing a ref does not schedule a new render.'],
['Compare stylesheets, inline style objects, classes, CSS modules, and conditional styling.','CSS module','A build-time convention that scopes imported class names to reduce accidental stylesheet collisions.','How do inline style objects differ from ordinary CSS?','React style props use JavaScript objects and generally camelCased property names. They express declarations for one element; selectors, pseudo-classes, media queries, and shared rules belong in suitable stylesheets or styling tools. Choose scope and maintenance needs before choosing a styling technique.'],
['Follow useState, effects, reducers, refs, memoization, callbacks, and custom hooks, while keeping render pure.','Effect cleanup','A function returned by an Effect setup to undo or stop the external synchronization established by that setup.','When does an Effect cleanup run?','React runs cleanup before rerunning an Effect with changed dependencies and when the component unmounts. Development checks may also perform an extra setup/cleanup cycle. Declare every reactive dependency used by setup and cleanup. Use event handlers for user actions and derive values during render when no external synchronization is needed.'],
['Map URLs to views, nested routes, parameters, navigation, and server fallback behavior; preserve the book’s router-version context.','Client-side routing','Selecting and navigating application views from a URL while keeping the current document active.','Why can a client-routed URL work after a click but fail on refresh?','A click can be handled by the client router, while refresh asks the server for that exact path. Configure the host to return the application entry for supported client routes, or use a routing strategy compatible with the host. Also verify the application base path and asset URLs.'],
['Place error boundaries around meaningful UI regions and distinguish render failures from event-handler and asynchronous failures.','Error boundary','A React component that catches supported errors in descendant rendering and lifecycle work and can display a fallback.','Does an error boundary catch every JavaScript exception?','No. A boundary handles supported descendant render and lifecycle failures, not arbitrary event-handler exceptions, asynchronous callbacks, or failures in the boundary itself. Handle those failures where they occur. Choose boundary granularity, provide an accessible fallback, and log enough context to diagnose the failure.'],
['Separate development and production artifacts, static hosting, base paths, routing, and validation of the deployed interface.','Production build','Deployable application output prepared by the selected build system, usually with optimized assets and environment-specific configuration.','Why are browser-exposed environment variables unsuitable for secrets?','A static browser application is delivered to users. Values embedded in its JavaScript or accessible network requests can be inspected. Keep private credentials on a trusted server and give the client only the capabilities it is authorized to use. Verify production URLs and caching after deployment.'],
['Inspect package scripts, dependencies, modules, JSX transformation, bundling, development serving, and output configuration.','Bundler','A build tool that resolves a module graph and emits assets according to its configured transformations and output strategy.','What should you explain before configuring a React toolchain from scratch?','Identify the entry module, dependency resolution, JSX transformation, styles and other assets, development server, production output, and host requirements. React is one dependency in that pipeline. Keep versions reproducible and verify that the emitted files work on the intended host.'],
['Represent loading, success, error, cancellation, cache identity, freshness, and out-of-order request completion explicitly.','Stale response race','An older request completing after a newer request and incorrectly replacing state intended for the newer selection.','How do you stop an older fetch result from overwriting a newer selection?','Associate each request with the selected key and ignore results after that request becomes obsolete, or cancel it where supported. Check response status and model errors and loading deliberately. A cache also needs stable keys and a freshness policy; it is not simply a global variable holding the last response.'],
['Use providers and consumers to share values across a subtree, while considering ownership and consumer updates.','Context provider','A component-tree boundary supplying a context value to descendants reading that context.','Does Context eliminate the need for state management?','Context distributes a value; state, reducers, and other stores can own and update that value. Define ownership and update frequency, and consider provider placement and value identity. Splitting unrelated concerns can limit unnecessary consumer work. Do not use context only to avoid one straightforward prop.'],
['Render into another DOM container while preserving membership in the React tree; manage modal focus and dismissal.','Portal','A way to render children into a different DOM container while retaining their logical React-tree relationships.','Why can an event inside a portal reach a React ancestor outside its DOM container?','A portal changes physical DOM placement, not its logical React ancestry. React events can propagate through that component ancestry. For a modal, portal placement also does not automatically provide focus trapping, accessible naming, background interaction control, or focus restoration.'],
['Use semantic controls, labels, focus management, keyboard behavior, contrast, and accessible feedback; test with assistive technology.','Accessible name','The name exposed to accessibility APIs for a control or other relevant element, derived by the platform naming rules.','Why is a clickable div usually a poor substitute for a button?','A native button supplies semantics, keyboard activation, focus behavior, and disabled behavior that a generic div does not provide automatically. Choose the native control matching the action, give it a clear name, and verify keyboard and screen-reader operation. Automated checks cover only part of accessibility.'],
['Connect further resources and next steps to the component, state, effects, testing, performance, and accessibility foundations already learned.','Learning transfer','Applying a concept and its assumptions to a new problem instead of relying on the exact example previously memorized.','How would you demonstrate that you understand a React concept beyond a tutorial?','Build a small feature, explain state ownership and data flow, test a failure and a changed requirement, and justify the implementation. Use the original book for its examples and the relevant current documentation for version-sensitive APIs. A working happy path alone does not demonstrate correct effects or accessibility.'],
['Use the complete index to find terms and cross-references without treating an index entry as a definition.','Index','An alphabetized lookup connecting terms to locations in the source book.','How should an index support interview revision?','Find a term, open its original explanation and example, and relate it to a concrete behavior or failure. Index page numbers refer to the printed book, while the full reader labels physical PDF pages. Do not infer a complete definition from an index heading alone.'],
['Retain the final supplied license text and distinguish source provenance from technical instruction.','Source license','The terms supplied with a work governing its permitted uses.','Why retain source attribution and license context?','They identify the work and the context in which it was supplied. They are reference material rather than a React mechanism. Keep technical explanations separate from claims about permissions and consult the actual terms for any proposed use.']
];
